#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111863 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we have an angle $\\theta$ in the coordinate plane positioned in the usual way. Its **reference angle**, denoted $\\theta_R$, is the smallest positive acute angle formed with the $x$-axis.\n\nFor example, let's consider the angles\n\n$$\n\\theta=45^\\circ, 135^\\circ, 225^\\circ\n$$\n\nand $315^\\circ$. These angles lie in the first, second, third, and fourth quadrants, respectively. And each of these angles has a reference angle\n\n$$\n\\theta_R = 45^\\circ\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/bf/bfba74ff2c6a69b3db1c9478f5caac4c31578ed93b47302a74d843b04fc6ee85.png)\n\n**Note:** Any reference angle always lies between $0^\\circ$ and $90^\\circ$ inclusively:\n\n$$\n0\\leq \\theta_R \\leq 90^\\circ\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "2338474e-8937-5e5e-88ae-7e7848bf2d6f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2492 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
